So, if you look at the capital equipment plan for public works,

00:50:55.270 --> 00:50:59.030
we've talked about purchasing a plow truck every five years.

00:50:59.795 --> 00:51:02.275
So, we maintain three trucks

00:51:02.435 --> 00:51:04.915
that are 15 years or older,

00:51:05.395 --> 00:51:08.515
and with the reserve truck that's less than,

00:51:10.275 --> 00:51:11.555
you know, 20 years old.

00:51:12.560 --> 00:51:15.600
That's been our goal. We've been working towards that

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:18.880
over since around 2014.

00:51:19.600 --> 00:51:22.080
So, bought one in 2014.

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:23.600
We bought one in

00:51:25.215 --> 00:51:27.775
basically the winter of twenty

00:51:27.775 --> 00:51:28.495
nineteen,

00:51:28.815 --> 00:51:30.655
and then we'll have the next one,

00:51:31.215 --> 00:51:32.575
the twenty three-twenty

00:51:32.575 --> 00:51:33.375
four budget

00:51:34.015 --> 00:51:37.535
cycle if we get on the bandwagon fairly quickly.

00:51:38.910 --> 00:51:39.550
So,

00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:43.790
would be the big ticket item that we would be looking at.

00:51:45.790 --> 00:51:46.510
You know,

00:51:46.910 --> 00:51:50.030
the fire department has indicated that

00:51:50.445 --> 00:51:56.925
they're probably going to present the town with a proposal for purchase of a rescue engine

00:51:57.645 --> 00:51:58.525
combo,

00:51:59.325 --> 00:52:05.770
which would basically take two of the large public works or not public works, large fire department vehicles

00:52:05.850 --> 00:52:09.290
out of service and replace it with a single combo unit.

00:52:10.010 --> 00:52:11.930
But they're pretty expensive units.

00:52:13.530 --> 00:52:17.785
You know, so I think the board has to anticipate that coming.

00:52:18.025 --> 00:52:20.105
Whether the Board of Finance

00:52:20.105 --> 00:52:21.785
and Board of Selectmen

00:52:21.785 --> 00:52:28.585
agree with it or not, that's up to you. But I think you will kind of see that from the fire department.

00:52:30.180 --> 00:52:32.820
I also think we should still consider

00:52:32.980 --> 00:52:35.620
a Bolt EUV for senior transportation.

00:52:36.420 --> 00:52:38.260
Just because I think this is,

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:43.220
you know, over the long term, it's a more economical way for us to be providing,

00:52:43.605 --> 00:52:45.525
you know, medical transport,

00:52:46.165 --> 00:52:47.685
which we do an awful lot of.

00:52:50.245 --> 00:52:52.165
So, and this would be roughly

00:52:52.165 --> 00:52:54.005
a $30,000

00:52:54.245 --> 00:52:55.045
expenditure

00:52:55.605 --> 00:52:57.685
if we considered it as a town.

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:00.640
So building maintenance,

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:02.800
you know, the community

00:53:02.800 --> 00:53:06.080
center, we are proceeding with the RFP review at this time.

00:53:06.800 --> 00:53:09.440
The assumption is that we have the funds currently

00:53:09.840 --> 00:53:12.480
from the ARPA funds and potentially

00:53:13.655 --> 00:53:16.295
some money from the Multi Use Building Fund.

00:53:17.335 --> 00:53:22.135
The construction of the senior transportation garage at 15 Center Street,

00:53:22.695 --> 00:53:24.775
that's largely grant funded.

00:53:25.255 --> 00:53:26.775
However, it is anticipating

00:53:26.775 --> 00:53:29.120
the use of about $75,000

00:53:29.120 --> 00:53:31.200
from the multi use building fund

00:53:31.280 --> 00:53:33.840
for the town's portion of that.

00:53:34.960 --> 00:53:37.280
The renovation of the old town hall,

00:53:38.240 --> 00:53:40.800
which will be done to satisfy the Connecticut,

00:53:41.280 --> 00:53:42.640
the SHPO office,

00:53:43.135 --> 00:53:44.655
So, we're able

00:53:44.655 --> 00:53:46.415
to tear down the other building.

00:53:47.135 --> 00:53:51.135
And then HVAC updates to the Andover Fire Department

00:53:51.455 --> 00:53:54.575
and then also to the town hall.

00:53:55.055 --> 00:53:58.095
The fire department would be paid for

00:53:58.300 --> 00:53:59.580
out of the,

00:54:01.260 --> 00:54:03.980
basically the fire department capital fund.

00:54:04.140 --> 00:54:09.580
The HVAC updates to the town hall would be paid for with LOSIP funds.

00:54:11.100 --> 00:54:12.460
You know, those are both would,

00:54:13.285 --> 00:54:15.845
they would technically be in

00:54:15.845 --> 00:54:16.645
probably

00:54:16.645 --> 00:54:18.485
in this budget year,

00:54:19.045 --> 00:54:23.365
but they're the next big, two big things coming up in terms of building maintenance.

00:54:25.685 --> 00:54:28.725
Next item up is bridges and culverts.

00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:32.980
So the replacement for Bunker Hill Bridge,

00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:36.420
you know, we've been very fortunate to secure

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:38.340
complete funding for that.

00:54:39.140 --> 00:54:42.660
We currently have around $450,000

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:44.340
in the bank

00:54:44.045 --> 00:54:46.445
to help pay for that.

00:54:47.085 --> 00:54:52.285
We're not going to be able to release that money because we'll have to pay that money to the contractor

00:54:52.765 --> 00:54:53.645
initially,

00:54:53.645 --> 00:54:55.885
but we will get fully reimbursed

00:54:56.285 --> 00:54:59.405
for that bridge as well as the Long Hill Bridge.

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:09.640
The good news is we should have enough swing money in that fund right now that we don't need to add anything in future budget years.

00:55:10.280 --> 00:55:15.720
And when both bridges are replaced, we should be able to roll that money back into the general fund,

00:55:16.575 --> 00:55:18.335
which is good. So

00:55:18.575 --> 00:55:23.295
we are proceeding with the design for the Hutchinson Road culvert replacement.

00:55:23.375 --> 00:55:25.295
I'm hoping to meet with the engineer

00:55:25.935 --> 00:55:26.815
next week

00:55:27.055 --> 00:55:30.175
to start the ball rolling again on that.

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:33.720
At this point, I'm anticipating

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:38.120
we're going to use the state local bridge design program for that,

00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:40.040
which provides 50%

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:40.760
funding.

00:55:41.240 --> 00:55:43.800
However, I've been hearing some rumors that

00:55:44.015 --> 00:55:45.775
I know some of the

00:55:46.175 --> 00:55:49.615
COGs and also the Council of Small Towns

00:55:49.775 --> 00:55:53.455
are pushing the state to use some of their federal,

00:55:55.615 --> 00:55:58.335
you know, COVID relief and federal

00:55:59.220 --> 00:56:00.900
IJA funds

00:56:01.060 --> 00:56:02.420
to backfill

00:56:02.420 --> 00:56:05.140
the culvert programs for the towns.

00:56:05.620 --> 00:56:11.620
So, want to get to the point that we can set ourselves up to take advantage of that if they do increase

00:56:12.765 --> 00:56:17.085
the percentage funding that the state will pay for those programs.

00:56:18.125 --> 00:56:21.405
If I can get the state to pay for all of that, that would be even better.

00:56:21.565 --> 00:56:25.405
But as it stands right now, we're looking at 50% funding for those.

00:56:26.630 --> 00:56:31.430
We have enough money sitting in the bank to do the Hutchinson Road culvert replacement.

00:56:32.790 --> 00:56:35.350
And then we kind of go from there and just keep

00:56:35.670 --> 00:56:38.630
doing them as we can fund that project.

00:56:40.115 --> 00:56:41.155
Roadways,

00:56:41.395 --> 00:56:44.195
I think we're basically going to continue

00:56:44.675 --> 00:56:46.595
with our existing program

00:56:46.835 --> 00:56:49.235
of shimming and chip sealing roads.

00:56:49.715 --> 00:57:03.320
We should again be working in the northern half of the town and fully complete shimming and chip sealing all the roads in the northern half of town. We're kind of working our way around the town counterclockwise.

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:07.960
And then we're also working on a trip grant

00:57:09.095 --> 00:57:13.575
that will cover the cost of reclaiming and repaving

00:57:14.135 --> 00:57:15.495
Skinner Hill Road

00:57:15.815 --> 00:57:17.335
and Hendy Road.

00:57:18.615 --> 00:57:20.615
So, you know, it's

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:29.770
a fairly long shot grant, but I'm sure there's going to be a lot of towns applying for it. But I think we can make a pretty good argument for why that should be funded.

00:57:31.450 --> 00:57:33.290
Next up, energy costs.

00:57:35.285 --> 00:57:36.085
Obviously,

00:57:36.725 --> 00:57:39.765
and oil have been incredibly expensive

00:57:40.165 --> 00:57:41.765
for a lot of people.

00:57:42.165 --> 00:57:45.605
We've been very fortunate that we've been locked in

00:57:45.765 --> 00:57:46.805
this year

00:57:47.045 --> 00:57:48.005
at around,

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:50.460
I think $2.29

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:53.580
a gallon for both diesel and heating oil.

00:57:54.620 --> 00:57:56.300
If we locked in today,

00:57:56.940 --> 00:57:59.420
we would be locking in at around $3.8

00:57:59.420 --> 00:58:03.820
a gallon for next year for heating oil as well as diesel.

00:58:04.345 --> 00:58:07.225
The price has been coming down a lot this fall.

00:58:07.465 --> 00:58:16.905
It seems like it's kind of bottomed out when it's going up a little bit down a little bit. If you look at it on a day to day basis. So my guess is it's about bottomed out.

00:58:17.625 --> 00:58:18.105
But,

00:58:18.665 --> 00:58:24.990
know, I don't know. Obviously, there's a lot of geopolitical factors that go into price of that too.

00:58:25.550 --> 00:58:27.230
But if we locked in today,

00:58:27.550 --> 00:58:30.670
we would still be looking at about a 40% increase

00:58:30.910 --> 00:58:32.670
in costs for

00:58:33.070 --> 00:58:35.070
both diesel and home heating oil

00:58:35.755 --> 00:58:37.915
compared to what we're paying now.

00:58:39.915 --> 00:58:40.795
Gasoline,

00:58:41.915 --> 00:58:45.195
budgeted last year, we budgeted about $4 a gallon.

00:58:45.595 --> 00:58:52.640
And when we started off in June, we were averaging about $4.30 a gallon, but obviously it's come under $4

00:58:53.520 --> 00:59:00.160
and, you know, we're currently paying, you know, $3.50 $3.40 sitting on the same chair. And it still

00:59:00.480 --> 00:59:03.965
seems to be coming down. So,

00:59:04.525 --> 00:59:09.565
I would say next year, my guess would be we would budget about $3.50

00:59:09.565 --> 00:59:10.445
a gallon.

00:59:10.765 --> 00:59:14.925
So, we should actually be able to reduce our costs a little bit compared to currently.

00:59:16.510 --> 00:59:17.790
Electricity,

00:59:18.190 --> 00:59:21.070
you know, it's very hard to predict because

00:59:21.390 --> 00:59:22.110
the

00:59:22.830 --> 00:59:24.430
utility companies

00:59:24.670 --> 00:59:25.550
basically,

00:59:26.590 --> 00:59:28.990
you know, they bid out in six month

00:59:29.495 --> 00:59:30.455
intervals

00:59:30.855 --> 00:59:31.575
and

00:59:31.815 --> 00:59:36.295
they haven't, there aren't any bids in for next year for electricity.

00:59:36.295 --> 00:59:38.295
So it's pretty tough to tell what

00:59:38.455 --> 00:59:42.215
our, you know, forecasts are electricity costs at this point.

00:59:43.175 --> 00:59:44.615
So that covers utilities.

00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:48.200
And the last thing that's kind of a big unknown

00:59:48.600 --> 00:59:49.320
is

00:59:50.120 --> 00:59:51.320
the state

00:59:51.320 --> 00:59:52.280
adopted

00:59:52.920 --> 00:59:54.280
a constitutional

00:59:54.520 --> 00:59:55.320
ability

00:59:55.560 --> 00:59:58.200
to hold early elections.

00:59:58.200 --> 01:00:01.175
In other words, to keep the polls open

01:00:01.655 --> 01:00:05.335
some length of time before the actual day of elections.

01:00:06.135 --> 01:00:07.415
But there's nothing

01:00:07.575 --> 01:00:08.615
right now

01:00:08.935 --> 01:00:12.215
about how that's going to be done or how it can be done.

01:00:13.920 --> 01:00:17.280
Where that comes where that's a concern to the town

01:00:17.440 --> 01:00:20.320
is we have pretty old voting machines.

01:00:21.040 --> 01:00:22.400
And if

01:00:22.480 --> 01:00:25.600
we end up having to use the voting machines,

01:00:26.765 --> 01:00:32.685
you know, and keep elections open for multiple days, you know, potentially even multiple weeks,

01:00:33.165 --> 01:00:37.565
our voting machines aren't capable of doing running tallies.

01:00:38.365 --> 01:00:42.690
So, we may actually be ending up looking at having to purchase new

01:00:44.530 --> 01:00:45.410
election,

01:00:45.730 --> 01:00:47.810
you know, counting machines

01:00:48.450 --> 01:00:50.210
depending on what the state

01:00:50.770 --> 01:00:51.730
decides.

01:00:52.850 --> 01:01:00.375
And, you know, I have no idea whether that's realistic or not. Just, I don't know where the state's going with that. So,

01:01:01.175 --> 01:01:02.855
in terms of the kind of overview,

01:01:03.255 --> 01:01:04.455
that's what I have.

01:01:05.175 --> 01:01:09.655
I'll send all this to you. I'll try to send this all to all the Board of Selected members tomorrow.

01:01:11.420 --> 01:01:14.540
You know, that's just all food for thought as we're deciding

01:01:14.700 --> 01:01:17.180
where we go with next year's budget.

01:01:18.540 --> 01:01:21.100
The good news is it's not nearly as bad as,

01:01:21.420 --> 01:01:23.500
you know, I thought it was going to be.

01:01:25.585 --> 01:01:29.345
We're now starting to look at locking in potentially

01:01:29.425 --> 01:01:30.545
heating oil

01:01:30.865 --> 01:01:32.945
in the low $3 a gallon,

01:01:33.345 --> 01:01:36.225
whereas a couple months ago we would have been locking in at

01:01:36.465 --> 01:01:37.745
something in the fives.

01:01:39.180 --> 01:01:40.300
So, we're definitely

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:41.340
better.

